lished">Sep 4, 2021</time></a></header><div class=3D"c-mention_content c-pr=
ose"><p>It adds the <code>baseurl</code> because it is helpful to know that=
 your file paths will be correct or not. Jekyll is emulating how the file p=
aths would need to be when your site is uploaded to <code>/blog</code>.</p>=
<p>If it stripped <code>/blog</code> from the URLs then you wouldn=E2=80=99=
t know if you had bad paths or links prior to pushing up to GitHub to host =
on Pages.</p><p>If you really don=E2=80=99t want to see <code>/blog</code> =
in your paths when running <code>jekyll serve</code> locally, then you can =
completely remove <code>basewebsite: /blog</code> from your _config.yml fil=
e. GitHub Pages automatically adds that value based on the repo and type of=
 site (<a href=3D"https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-git=
hub-pages/about-github-pages#types-of-github-pages-sites" rel=3D"noopener">=
user or project site</a>).</p><p>For example if you have a repo named <code=
>blog</code> it would be a project site and GitHub would automatically add =
<code>basewebsite: /blog</code> when building your Jekyll site.</p></div></=
div></article></li><li class=3D"c-mentions_list-item"><article id=3D"mentio=
